So the overload problem from Kaspa syncing can be solved by not saving all headers on-chain.

Instead, just store one Merkle root per batch (like per Canxium block or every X minutes), off-chain. Then miners submit only proofs (Merkle branch) to prove their Kaspa block is valid and was included.

I made a quick Solidity example where you store a Merkle root and verify block inclusion via a Merkle proof. It saves space and still keeps trustless validation. All the heavy stuff (Kaspa block scanning, hash aggregation) stays off-chain. You only store the compact root and validate proofs if needed.

If you want, I can send a Python script to generate Merkle proofs and hashes too.

